## Increment 3 of the concurrency adoption (§4.1 piece 3 — cooperative
## cancellation). A leaf future observes a cooperative `cx.cancelled?` bit at
## its poll (await) boundary and EARLY-RETURNS instead of parking forever.
## That early return lets the awaiting `async def` state machine COMPLETE,
## and its scope-exit drop then fires the Increment-2 state-indexed drop glue
## so the leaf sub-future's `def drop` (its reactor slot / fd analogue) is
## cleaned up.
##
## Surface (honest, current-lowering): the cancel bit is a THREAD-LOCAL flag,
## like the reactor handle (`ruxen_reactor_current_handle`) futures already
## read from inside poll — because the user async body does NOT see `cx`
## (only the synthesised `poll(cx)` does). `ruxen_request_cancel()` sets it,
## `cx.cancelled?` (and the free fn `ruxen_cancel_requested()`) read it,
## `ruxen_clear_cancel()` resets it. The flag is thread-scoped and PERSISTS
## (it is NOT auto-cleared at block_on entry) — that is why a cancel requested
## BEFORE block_on (as `main` does here) is observed by the first poll.
## COOPERATIVE: a future only stops at a point where it CHECKS the bit; it does
## NOT preempt an await-free compute loop.
##
## Shape: main requests cancel, then block_on's a `worker` async fn that
## awaits a `CancelAware` leaf. The leaf's first poll sees `cx.cancelled?` and
## returns Ready(-1) immediately (instead of Pending forever). worker
## completes returning -1; `puts` prints it; then at main's scope exit the
## block_on root future is dropped (the §4.1 block_on-exit drop), firing
## CancelAware's `def drop` via the Increment-2 state-indexed glue. Hence the
## drop line prints AFTER "worker returned -1".

class CancelAware
  tag: Int
  include Future
  type Output = Int

  def init(t: Int)
    self.tag = t
  end

  def self.make(t: Int) -> CancelAware
    CancelAware.new(t)
  end

  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Int]
    # Cooperative cancel check at the await boundary.
    if cx.cancelled?
      Poll.Ready(-1)
    else
      Poll.Pending
    end
  end

  def drop
    puts "CancelAware dropped tag=#{self.tag}"
  end
end

async def worker() -> Int
  let v = CancelAware.make(7).await
  v
end

def main
  # An external cancel signal arrives before we start driving the worker.
  ruxen_request_cancel()
  let r = block_on(worker())
  puts "worker returned #{r}"
end
